# =============================================================================
# JudicialSync — the single TLS origin
# =============================================================================
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AT ALL.
#
# OIDC validation compares the `iss` claim in a token against the issuer the
# relying party discovered. If the browser talks to Keycloak at one URL and the
# API container talks to it at another, those two strings differ and every
# login fails with an error that points at the token rather than at the
# network. The usual local-dev workarounds — `localhost:8080` for the browser,
# `keycloak:8080` for the API — produce exactly that mismatch.
#
# The fix is to give Keycloak ONE address that is correct from both sides.
# `judicialsync.localhost` resolves to loopback in the browser (RFC 6761
# reserves `.localhost`, and `scripts/ensure-hosts.sh` covers resolvers that
# do not implement the wildcard), and inside the Compose network a network
# alias on this proxy service makes the same name resolve to Caddy. One name,
# one scheme, one port, one issuer.
#
# TLS is terminated here and ONLY here. `TechArch/04-security.md` §7.5 and
# `FRD/F13` require TLS 1.2+ in transit "with no fallback to unencrypted
# transport": neither `api` nor `keycloak` publishes a host port, so there is
# no plaintext path to either from outside the Compose network (threat T-01-12).
#
# The certificate comes from Caddy's internal CA (`tls internal`). The API
# container TRUSTS that CA by importing its root certificate — it does not
# disable verification. `NODE_TLS_REJECT_UNAUTHORIZED` is never set anywhere in
# this repository; see threat T-01-13.
#
# Production terminates TLS at the cloud gateway with a real certificate. That
# deployment IaC is deliberately deferred (Phase 1 CONTEXT), which is precisely
# why this file must not grow any production-shaped configuration.
# =============================================================================

{
	# Caddy would otherwise open :80 and emit HTTP->HTTPS redirects. There is no
	# plaintext listener in this stack at all, so there is nothing to redirect
	# from, and binding :80 would create the very path T-01-12 forbids.
	auto_https disable_redirects

	# Local development only. An internally-issued certificate does not need an
	# ACME account, and this keeps Caddy from reaching for the network.
	email admin@judicialsync.invalid
}

judicialsync.localhost:8443 {
	tls internal

	encode gzip

	log {
		output stdout
		format console
	}

	# --- Identity provider -----------------------------------------------
	# Keycloak runs with KC_HTTP_RELATIVE_PATH=/auth, so its own routes already
	# begin with /auth and the prefix is passed through unchanged. Combined
	# with KC_HOSTNAME=https://judicialsync.localhost:8443/auth, the issuer
	# Keycloak advertises is byte-identical to the URL both the browser and the
	# API container use to reach it.
	handle /auth/* {
		reverse_proxy keycloak:8080
	}

	# --- Platform Core API -------------------------------------------------
	# The API sets a global `/api/v1` prefix (apps/api/src/main.ts), so the
	# prefix is passed through rather than stripped.
	handle /api/* {
		reverse_proxy api:3000
	}

	# --- Everything else ---------------------------------------------------
	# Plan 01-13 replaces this block with: reverse_proxy web:5173
	handle {
		respond "JudicialSync Platform Core. UI ships in plan 01-13. API: /api/v1/health" 200
	}
}
